{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head a19f9c86de901daed814c762bc8ed0c07e4cdfbb on task/release-readiness-review-repair.\n\nThe finding I raised is genuinely closed, and closed better than I asked. One narrow condition of this request does not hold, and the request's own terms say that means changes-requested. The fix is one assertion.\n\nMY EARLIER FINDING IS FIXED, and the fix is honest rather than cosmetic. The false sentence is gone. SECURITY.md now says plainly that GitHub Issues are public and suitable for ordinary support questions, feature requests and non-sensitive bug reports, links them, and keeps the prohibition on putting a vulnerability, exploit, credential, private repository content or personal data in an issue or a workroom. That matches the repository as configured: public, issues enabled.\n\nIt also closes the structural point I raised rather than only the sentence. The new paragraph states that repository visibility, issue availability and private vulnerability reporting are GitHub settings outside this source tree, that source CI cannot guarantee them, and that they must be rechecked before the first public push. That is the right resolution: the gap between documented boundary and configured reality is named as a limitation instead of being papered over, and a reader is told where the guarantee stops.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED AT THIS HEAD. Checkout clean and exact, parents 346ffb108c798dd193e3324ae4753c082117d9dc and the reviewed candidate 353314480887a1e31927551a568b15daec8842c1, current main now an ancestor - which also clears the fourteen-commits-behind observation from my previous verdict. True scope against main is the same seven files, 322 insertions, no product behaviour change. Gates: make vet, make test, make race, make build, npm test 42 of 42, make ui, make spike, git diff --exit-code clean, and the fresh-clone gate passes. HEAD confirmed identical before and after every measurement.\n\nThe surface gate is load-bearing for three of the four distinctions I probed. Replacing \"GitHub Issues are public.\" with a vaguer sentence: caught. Deleting the external-settings limitation paragraph entirely: caught. Repointing the [issues] link at another host: caught.\n\nMUST FIX, and it is one line. The fourth mutation survived. I replaced \"not open a public issue for a security report.\" with \"not worry about it.\" and the whole suite stayed green.\n\nThat sentence is the security policy's central instruction. The invitation to use Issues is pinned and the private advisory link is pinned, but the prohibition that joins them is not, so the clause that actually protects the boundary can be deleted or inverted and CI will not notice. This request asked me to \"confirm public_surface_test.go makes those distinctions load-bearing\", and for the distinction that directs vulnerabilities privately, it does not.\n\nI am raising it rather than waving it through because it is the same failure mode that produced this branch. Last round a security claim drifted from reality and no gate objected; the response was to make the claims checkable. A pinned invitation with an unpinned prohibition leaves exactly half of that pair checkable. Add the prohibition to the required fragments in public_surface_test.go and this closes.\n\nNOT FINDINGS, carried forward from my previous verdict and still true, recorded so they are not lost between rounds.\n\nSecret scanning, secret-scanning push protection and Dependabot security updates all read back disabled on the public repository. None is a stated condition. Push protection is cheap and fits this project's own concern that credentials never reach the record; worth a decision before the first push rather than after.\n\nReachable history carries Hugh Pyle \u003chughpyle@gmail.com\u003e as author and committer throughout. That is the owner's own address and his to publish, but publication makes it permanent, and a history privacy and authorship audit should state it rather than leave a reader to find it.\n\nPrivate vulnerability reporting still reads back enabled true, which I rechecked directly at this head rather than carrying the earlier result forward.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:987e6b20ab1d5da8ed780325f3a863948b438f65","head":"a19f9c86de901daed814c762bc8ed0c07e4cdfbb","verdict":"changes-requested"}}